From: Ericsunny <noreply@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: cif: reject NULL input fmt in rkcif_set_fmt

`get_input_fmt()` returns NULL when the terminal sensor's `get_fmt` fails
(it logs "sensor fmt invalid, set to default size" and falls back to a
default rect, but still returns NULL).  `rkcif_set_fmt()` assigns that
NULL to `cif_fmt_in` and later dereferences it without a check:

    cif_fmt_in = get_input_fmt(dev->terminal_sensor.sd,
                               &input_rect, stream->id, &vc);
    stream->cif_fmt_in = cif_fmt_in;
    ...
    /* cif_fmt_in->mbus_code used below */

Observed on RK3568 (YC_RK3568_MB1V0, 4.19.232 BSP) with an OV8858 on the
CIF chain: when the sensor subdev rejected the query (pad handling), boot
died at `rkcif_set_fmt+0x2cc` with a NULL pointer dereference, killing
PID 1 and panicking the board ("Attempted to kill init").

`rkcif_sanity_check_fmt()` already checks this return value; make
`rkcif_set_fmt()` do the same and fall back to the first supported input
format so a single bad subdev response cannot take down the boot.

This is a vendor-code robustness fix, independent of the sensor side.
Sensor-side root cause is fixed in ov8858_sm (#52); this patch is not
required for that fix and is not deployed in the current image.

Apply from the kernel tree root:
    git apply driver/patches/rkcif-null-check.patch
(or `patch -p1 < ...`).

Signed-off-by: Ericsunny <noreply@example.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/rockchip/cif/capture.c
@@ -3279,6 +3279,16 @@ static void rkcif_set_fmt(struct rkcif_stream *stream,
 		cif_fmt_in = get_input_fmt(dev->terminal_sensor.sd,
 			      &input_rect, stream->id, &vc);
 		stream->cif_fmt_in = cif_fmt_in;
+
+		/*
+		 * get_input_fmt() returns NULL when the sensor's get_fmt
+		 * fails; dereferencing it below kills PID 1 at boot.
+		 * Fall back to the first supported input format instead.
+		 */
+		if (!cif_fmt_in) {
+			v4l2_err(&dev->v4l2_dev,
+				 "input fmt invalid, use default input fmt\n");
+			cif_fmt_in = &in_fmts[0];
+			stream->cif_fmt_in = cif_fmt_in;
+		}
 	}
 
 	if (dev->terminal_sensor.sd) {
